Wire Flutter's scale recognizer (0015) into touch dispatch: two or more fingers on an `on_pinch` element pinch it.

What:
- `PinchEvent` gains `touch: Option<TouchPinch>`: `None` for a trackpad pinch (every backend's literal sets it), for
  a touch pinch `TouchPinch::Start` / `Update` / `End` carrying Flutter's `ScaleStartDetails` / `ScaleUpdateDetails`
  / `ScaleEndDetails` (0015's `gpui::scale`). A touch pinch also fills the trackpad fields: `position` is the focal
  point, `delta` the scale's change since the last event, `phase` Started / Moved / Ended, so code written for
  trackpad zoom zooms with fingers too.
- `gestures/pinch.rs`: `PinchArena`, Flutter's gesture arena between one element's `ScaleGestureRecognizer` and the
  window's touch recognizer, as an explicit state (`Idle`, `Single`, `Contested`, `Won`). One finger stays the touch
  recognizer's (its pan slop is below the scale's, so it always wins in Flutter's arena too); a second finger on the
  same element hands both to the scale recognizer; when the scale passes its slop (`gpui::slops` from the window's
  `GestureTuning::touch_slop`, the one source), the touch recognizer gets `Cancelled` for them (a pan ends
  `Cancelled`, a pending tap never fires) and they reach only the pinch until they lift. Back to one finger before
  that, the scale gives up and waits for a second. A finger joining or leaving ends and restarts the gesture, as in
  Flutter; the last finger keeps it (one-pointer scale).
- Claims decide which element pinches, as Flutter's arena does: `TouchRoute::Gestures` carries the pinch element a
  touch joined. `Window::join_pinch(touch, owner)` (public, for custom elements) joins it, unless a deeper element
  claimed the touch (`on_touch`) or joined its own pinch; a deeper element that only left it to gestures (a button)
  keeps its taps, the pinch joins anyway. `Window::pinch_owner()` names the element a touch pinch is being dispatched
  to; the window dispatches the events with a synthesized touch `pointer_source`.
- div: `on_pinch` on an element with an id joins the touches that start over it (and leaves them to gestures, so an
  ancestor's `on_touch` cannot take them); its listeners get the touch pinch wherever the fingers move, only on that
  element, with `local_focal_point` relative to its bounds. Trackpad pinches keep upstream's hover rule.
- `scale.rs`: Flutter's `localFocalPoint` restored as `local_focal_point` on the start and update details (the
  recognizer sets the focal point; the receiving element makes it local, standing in for Flutter's transform).
- `recognizer_tests.rs`: the trace player is split out of `replay` as `play`, shared with the pinch tests.

Why: GPUI recognizes no pinch from touches (gestures.rs: "Pinch recognition is not implemented yet") and ignores
every finger after the first; touch platforms need pinch-to-zoom. The recognizer is Flutter's (0015); this is only
the glue, so it lives where the touch recognizer and element dispatch are. Reusing upstream's `PinchEvent` and
`on_pinch` keeps one listener for trackpad and touch pinches instead of a parallel API.

Depends on: 0004 (touch routes and claims), 0013 (`gpui::slops`, the trace harness), 0014 (the tuning), 0015
(the scale recognizer).

Test: `gestures::pinch::tests::touch_pinches_reach_the_deepest_on_pinch_element` (pinch_tests.rs), recorded traces
through the test platform into nested `on_pinch` divs: pinch out, pinch in on the nested element (deepest wins, local
focal point), rotate, a third finger joining, a finger lifted mid-pinch, a second finger turning a pan into a pinch,
a two-finger tap, one finger left before the slop, two fingers on an element without `on_pinch` (unchanged).

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

